WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Our final speaker of the day is becoming a Hillsdale regular.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.700
 He has spoken numerous
times at Hillsdale events not only on land

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:13.360
but also at sea during a Hillsdale College
cruise. His engagements on land include a

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.080
speech in October 2011 dedicating the statue
of Ronald Reagan on the Hillsdale campus.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:24.140
We are delighted that he is able to speak
for the college once again. Andrew Roberts

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.789
is a historian and best selling author of
several books. Among his most recent are A

00:00:28.789 --> 00:00:34.390
History of English Speaking People Since 1900
which won the Intercollegiate Studies Institute

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:39.540
Prize, Masters and Commanders: How Roosevelt,
Churchill, Marshall and Alanbrooke Won the

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:45.020
War in the West, which won the Emory Reeves
Prize and The Storm of War: A New History

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:49.350
Of The Second World War, which won the British
Army Military Book of the Year Award and rose

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:55.000
to number two on the Sunday New York Times
Bestseller List. His most recent book is Napoleon:

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.890
A Life.
It won a 2014 Grand Prix History Prize from

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:06.890
an organization who's name will test my very
rusty French language skills, the Fondation

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.490
Napoleon. The book was also very recently,
just a couple of days ago, the winner of the

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:19.390
LA Times Biography prize and Andrew tells
me just at lunch today that it's been optioned

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:26.390
for a Harvey Weinstein television series.
Dr. Roberts is a fellow the the Royal Society

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:32.150
of Literature, a trustee of the Margaret Thatcher
Archive Trust and a director of the Harry

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:37.700
Frank Guggenheim Foundation. Our final lecture
topic of this Hillsdale College National Leadership

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:43.610
Seminar is Churchill and the Historians. Please
welcome Dr. Andrew Roberts.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:50.610
Dr. Roberts: Ladies and gentlemen it's a great
honor to be invited to address you this afternoon

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:05.490
and thank you very much indeed Tim for those
kind words. It's perfectly true that my book

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:12.490
got to number two on the bestseller list,
beaten only by a book about Michael Jackson.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:20.780
In answer to the question that was posed to
James Muller about Churchill's religious sensibilities,

00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:28.849
he of course said that although he didn't
believe that Jesus Christ was the son of God

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:36.629
he did believe that he was the greatest man
who ever lived and he described himself, Churchill,

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:42.160
as acting like a flying buttress for the Church
of England in that he supported the church

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:47.140
but from the outside.
I'd like to take you back to the afternoon

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:53.140
of Tuesday, the twenty-sixth of April, 1927,
when as Chancellor of the Exchequer, Winston

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:59.129
Churchill presented that year's budget. He'd
already covered the changes in the taxation

00:02:59.129 --> 00:03:06.129
on sugar and bedding and silk and car licenses
and tea. You people will recall that the British

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:14.650
used to impose very light taxes on tea. Before
he got around to the subject of the taxation

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:21.459
of wine it is absolutely necessary to invoke
the great name of Mr. Gladstone said Churchill,

00:03:21.459 --> 00:03:26.409
a name which is received with reverence below
the gangway on the opposition side and with

00:03:26.409 --> 00:03:30.769
a certain amount of respect by honorable members
who sit opposite. At that point some MP's

00:03:30.769 --> 00:03:35.599
called out, "What about yourself?" Which,
considering that Churchill had only rejoined

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:40.670
the Tory party two years earlier, might have
been an awkward moment for him, an awkward

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:45.480
question to answer for someone who'd been
a senior minister in the last liberal ministry.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:51.709
"I occupy the impartial position of the historian,"
Churchill said diplomatically before moving

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:57.720
on to the details of his proposals.
As an historian himself, Churchill was fascinated

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:04.060
by the subjects of history and mentioned history
and historians often in speeches. "How strange

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:09.890
it is that the past is so little understood
and so quickly forgotten," he said in April

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:16.130
1929. We live in the most thoughtless of ages,
everyday headlines and short views. I've tried

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.370
to drag history up a little nearer to our
own times in case it should be helpful as

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:25.919
a guide in present difficulties. A quarter
of a century later he was saying much the

00:04:25.919 --> 00:04:31.310
same thing when after a luncheon to celebrate
the present queen's coronation he told an

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:37.509
American schoolboy who was on an ESU scholarship,
"Study history, study history. In history

00:04:37.509 --> 00:04:43.190
lies all the secrets of state craft."
The adjective historic also crops up very

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:47.190
often in his speeches and the future verdict
of history, not least on his own career and

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:52.800
achievements of course, clearly mattered to
him greatly. The quality he most often attributed

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.780
to historians of the future, over-optimistically
as it turned out in many cases regarding the

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:04.090
recording of his own doings, was impartiality.
He himself had a harsh early experience of

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:08.740
what happened when historians were not impartial.
After he had fallen in love with the written

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:14.250
style of the Whig historian Lord Macauley
learning large parts of Macauley's Lays of

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:21.250
Ancient Rome by heart. He was forced to do
this at school. Then out spake brave Horatius,

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.330
The Captain of the gate: “To every man upon
this earth death cometh soon or late. And

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:30.830
how can man die better than facing fearful
odds for the ashes of his fathers and the

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:35.949
temples of his gods. Then out spake Spurius
Lartius, a warrior proud was he. I shall stand

00:05:35.949 --> 00:05:40.169
at thy right hand and hold the bridge with
thee." He wasn't the only person who forced

00:05:40.169 --> 00:05:45.979
to learn it at school.
"I accepted all that Macauley wrote as gospel,"

00:05:45.979 --> 00:05:50.020
recalled Churchill, "and I was grieved to
read his harsh judgement upon the Great Duke

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:56.680
of Marlborough." Tellingly, the G in Great
Duke is capitalized in Churchill's text. There

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:02.380
was no on at hand to tell me that this historian,
ie. Macauley, with his captivating style and

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:07.300
devastating self-confidence was the prince
of literary rogues that always preferred the

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:13.220
tale to the truth and smirched all glorified
great men and garbled documents according

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:20.220
as they affected his drama. So perhaps the
outrageous lack of impartiality in the history

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:25.000
of Winston Churchill himself is not something
that would have shocked him however much it

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:29.970
might shock us.
In 1940's, 1950's and really up until his

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:35.960
death in 1965, Churchill was treated with
great respect by biographers and historians,

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:40.669
not least because they new it first hand what
he had done to protect their freedom. Isaiah

00:06:40.669 --> 00:06:47.669
Berlin, Philip Guedalla, Violet Bonham Carter,
Leslie Rowse, Charles Eade, Virginia Coles,

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:52.509
many others of these early memoirists and
biographers tended to write of him with affection

00:06:52.509 --> 00:06:58.710
and high regard. Event the first crack in
the edifice of positive recollections, the

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:04.830
Diaries of Lord Alanbrooke as presented in
two volumes by Arthur Bryant in 1957 and 1959

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:11.190
cut out several of Alanbrooke's harsher wartime
comments from his almost universally caustic

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:16.310
diaries.
Since the 1980's however, and certainly in

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:22.050
the 1990's a new revisionist school of history
has sprung up around Churchill which all too

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:28.340
often has sought to impose present day values
on words and actions of Churchill from an

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:35.340
entirely different age. This has taken place
simulaneously, both from the left and the

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:42.139
political right. On the left you have Clive
Ponting who as Richard mentioned earlier in

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:49.139
his speech, comes up with a lot of baloney
about Churchill having known about Pearl Harbor,

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:57.879
being an alcoholic, being responsible for
the death of and so on. Christopher Hitchens,

00:07:58.789 --> 00:08:04.440
much harder to pin down on the right or the
left, but he too pretty much embraced all

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:10.629
of the anti-Churchill theories going. There
was one article where he wrote in the Atlantic

00:08:10.629 --> 00:08:16.800
Monthly no fewer than twenty-three separate
accusations, many of them naughty conspiracy

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:23.800
theories which were pretty much all proven
to be inaccurate.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:30.810
Then there was Margaret Cook, the wife of
Robin Cook, the British Foreign Secretary,

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:37.080
Labor Foreign Secretary who wrote a book about
Winston Churchill being, quote, almost homosexual.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:42.880
I've no idea what almost homosexual means
by the way and I attended both a British boarding

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:49.880
school and Cambridge University.
You then have the whole issue of race. This

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:58.510
is the new attack on Winston Churchill, again
as Richard mentioned, the Bengal Famine is

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:05.820
brought up. There's a book by a man called
Muckety and of course Richard Toye's book

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:11.480
on imperialism which basically argued that
he was such a racist that he didn't care about

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:18.480
the deaths of black and brown people and in
the case of the Bengal Famine he as good as

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:24.600
encouraged it. In a review of Richard Toye's
book on Churchill's imperialism in the Times

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:31.269
Literary supplement, written by Johan Harry
it was asserted that during the Bengal Famine

00:09:31.269 --> 00:09:37.029
Churchill quote, refused to offer any aid
for months while hundreds of thousands died,

00:09:37.029 --> 00:09:42.220
unquote. The fact that those months actually
took place during the second World War when

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:48.600
India was trying to fight the Japanese who
had pretty much captured the whole of Burma

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:53.300
by then is not mentioned.
In the same review it's stated that President

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:59.399
Obama's grandfather Hussein Onyango Obama
quote, was imprisoned without trial for two

00:09:59.399 --> 00:10:06.399
years and tortured on Churchill's watch for
resisting Churchill's empire. Now closer examination

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:12.339
of the facts show that his imprisonment actually
took place before Churchill became Prime Minister.

00:10:12.339 --> 00:10:18.480
He wasn't tortured and he hadn't been in prison
for resisting imperialism either. Was Churchill

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:25.480
a racist? Yes, of course he was. Any men,
even men of the left, such as H.G. Wells were,

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:32.760
in those days, they believed biological racism
was accepted as a scientific fact. To criticize

00:10:32.829 --> 00:10:37.920
Churchill for this is a bit like criticizing
people for ignorance for thinking the sun

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:42.250
moved around the earth before the time of
Galileo. Anyhow, and this is the fact that

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:47.560
most people ignore or deny about Churchill's
overt racism is that it lead him to want to

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:53.800
protect native peoples and because he believed
the British had a profound imperial duty towards

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:56.529
them.
I've just been rereading Churchill's 1908

00:10:56.529 --> 00:11:03.040
book My African Journey about the time that
he spent in Egypt and the Sudan and Kenya

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:08.140
and Uganda and Tanzania the previous year
and virtually every paragraph has something

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:12.839
that is quite rightly completely outrageous
and unsayable nowadays about the backwardness,

00:11:12.839 --> 00:11:17.550
as he perceived it, of the native peoples
but the very next paragraph, again and again,

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:22.649
explains the sacred duties of the British
Empire as a result of this. Plucked pretty

00:11:22.649 --> 00:11:27.779
much entirely at random for example, here
are his musings about the Kenyan tribes.

00:11:27.779 --> 00:11:34.300
He wrote, "It's unquestionably an advantage
that the East African Negro should develop

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:40.950
a taste for civilized attire. His life will
gradually be made more complicated, more varied,

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:46.560
less crudely animal and himself raised to
a higher level of economic utility. A government

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:51.769
runs risks when it intrudes upon the domain
of fashion but when a veritable abyss of knowledge

00:11:51.769 --> 00:11:57.730
and science separates the ruler from the ruled,
authority is dealing with a native race still

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:03.019
plunged in it's primary squalor, without religion,
without clothes, without morals but willing

00:12:03.019 --> 00:12:08.950
to emerge and capable of emerging, such risks
may be fairly accepted."

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:15.730
At the Kikuyu he wrote, "No one can travel
even for a little while among the Kikuyu tribes

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:21.950
without acquiring a liking for these lighthearted
tractable, if brutish children or without

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:26.190
feeling that they're capable of being instructed
and raised from their present degradation.

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:31.870
It will be an ill day for these native races
when their fortunes are removed from the impartial

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:37.800
and august administration of the crown and
abandoned to the fierce self-interest of a

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:44.470
small white population." So much for the anti-Churchill
historians of the left and the achingly politically

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:49.019
correct with their chronologically challenged
racial critique of Churchill.

00:12:49.019 --> 00:12:53.709
What about the attacks from the right and
in David Irving's case the ultra-right? One

00:12:53.709 --> 00:12:59.820
rather hesitates to append the designation
historian to some of these people especially

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:06.820
Irving. Three who are undoubtedly intelligent
historians from the right, one that was mentioned

00:13:07.959 --> 00:13:13.529
earlier by Larry, John Trombley, another two,
the late Morris Cowling and Allen Clark. All

00:13:13.529 --> 00:13:18.579
argued that Churchill increased the rate of
collapse of the British empire by indulging

00:13:18.579 --> 00:13:24.760
in an essentially unnecessary war in 1939.
It ought to be unnecessary for me to re-fight

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:28.779
the argument over why that would have been
absolutely disastrous to have made peace with

00:13:28.779 --> 00:13:35.740
Hitler in 1940 or '41, but I'm happy to, in
questions and answers if necessary.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:42.740
Then you have the extreme libertarians. A
man called Robert Rako at the University of,

00:13:44.329 --> 00:13:48.410
is there such a thing as the University of
Buffalo or is a university that's in Buffalo?

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:55.100
One or the other, anyway. There he is and
he has said that Winston Churchill was a war

00:13:55.100 --> 00:14:02.100
criminal, a stooge of Stalin and a drug addict.
I find that people like that rarely take refuge

00:14:03.639 --> 00:14:09.139
in understatement.
From neither the right nor the left comes

00:14:09.139 --> 00:14:16.139
Mr. Nicholson Baker in a book called Human
Smoke. Mr. Baker says that Winston Churchill

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:23.209
was as bad as Hitler. He accuses him of being
an anti-semite, of using gas against Iraqi

00:14:23.209 --> 00:14:27.850
tribesman, the oldest one in the book and
of course anyone who can't tell the difference

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:34.850
between tear gas and mustard gas should not
be writing history books. Mr. Baker says in

00:14:35.279 --> 00:14:42.079
his introduction, "I used Wikipedia during
the writing of this book." This is a quote

00:14:42.079 --> 00:14:49.079
from him saying, "especially to check facts."
His previous two books, prior to this book

00:14:49.589 --> 00:14:56.589
on Churchill were on phone sex and masturbation.
Now I don't for a moment ladies and gentlemen

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:04.130
deny that Mr. Nicholson Baker might have been
a world expert on both of his pastimes. It

00:15:09.889 --> 00:15:16.889
doesn't do to separate a man from his hobbies
but he does nothing at all about Winston Churchill.

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:25.070
In these cases it's always best to stay calm
and to go back to the one historian one can

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:31.019
always trust, the late Sir Martin Gilbert.
Martin's biography of Churchill has been described

00:15:31.019 --> 00:15:34.699
as the longest biography ever written. At
ten million words it is the longest biography

00:15:34.699 --> 00:15:41.699
ever written, still it's not a page too long.
Wimps can always read the condensed version

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:47.370
that came out in 1992. This might be a good
time by the way to pay tribute to the noble

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:53.000
job that Larry Arnn and his team at Hillsdale
are doing in completing that work. There is

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:58.480
a literary form of apostolic succession that
starts with Randolph Churchill, hands over

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:05.480
the baton to Martin and then Martin handing
over to Larry. Once the last of the companion

00:16:06.910 --> 00:16:13.759
volumes are published, the Churchill, Gilbert,
Arnn work will stand as a gleaming monument

00:16:13.759 --> 00:16:20.759
to literature and scholarship so long as the
English tongue is spoken.

00:16:21.279 --> 00:16:28.279
But even the great Martin Gilbert is not without
his detractors. One was Robert Rhodes James

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:36.170
the editor of eight volumes of Churchill's
speeches. In his Times Literary review of

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:41.279
Martin's autobiography in praise of Churchill,
wonderful book that was published in June

00:16:41.279 --> 00:16:48.279
1994, Rhodes James wrote, quote "Gilbert's
was too bland adulatory and only too open

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:53.800
to counter attack." He means the original
biography, the official biography.

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:59.690
"The emptiness that I feel is that in his
fervor of activity in compiling and publishing

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:04.740
this great archive Gilbert never stands back
and reflects." "This strange and worryingly

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:11.410
narcissistic book", he writes that Gilbert
was never even met Churchill is less important

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:18.410
than he has no political experience." He said
Gilbert's book "conveys an impression of pettiness"

00:17:18.919 --> 00:17:23.329
and said, "Gilbert has devoted much of his
life to the search of Churchill and had done

00:17:23.329 --> 00:17:28.030
so honorably and with much devotion and dedication
but the fact that he has failed to come close

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:32.910
to his subject is much more evident to others
than to him. My complaint is that he never

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:37.049
really attempted to find the man only the
documents. There is more to biography than

00:17:37.049 --> 00:17:41.770
legwork and paperwork. These are the essential
prerequisites, the most difficult part of

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:48.140
the biographer's real task, the portrait of
a soul through his adventures through life."

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:55.140
The very next week in the Times Literary supplement,
the commentator Edward Luttwak replied superbly

00:17:55.280 --> 00:18:02.280
to that attack on Martin by Rhodes James.
He writes, "How true were the subject a romantic

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:08.799
poet, a reclusive painter or even a minor
politician but Winston Churchill the last

00:18:08.799 --> 00:18:15.250
I heard was sometimes engaged in public life,
sometimes holding offices of high consequence,

00:18:15.250 --> 00:18:19.500
in circumstances that sometimes endowed his
decisions and actions with some importance

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:25.340
to some people beyond the immediate circle
of his family, friends and personal employees.

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:29.559
That is the aspect of Churchill's life that
Gilbert's gigantic labors have recovered from

00:18:29.559 --> 00:18:34.580
many sources and most thoroughly reconstructed
for all of us, but an aspect evidently of

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:40.080
trivial import for Mr. Robert Rhodes James
who would obviously have preferred a speculative

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:46.600
essay on Churchill's inner life, perhaps illuminated
by the always solidly reliable insights of

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:53.000
psycho-analysis."
To those criticisms of Rhodes James I'd like

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:59.190
to add some of my own. To attack Martin Gilbert
for narcissism is a disgraceful assertion

00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:04.610
as anyone who knew this deeply modest and
self-effacing man will attest. He was writing

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:09.750
and autobiography which per force must make
references to the subject. There's a world

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:14.480
of difference between writing about oneself
in an autobiography and the kind of self love

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:19.440
summed up by the word narcissism. I defy anyone
to find a sentence in that book that could

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:26.360
justly be described narcissistic. I knew Rhodes
James a bit in the early 1980's when he was

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:31.230
the president of the Cambridge University
Conservative Association when I was it's chairman.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:36.669
That reference about Gilbert having no political
experience was really just a boast that Robert

00:19:36.669 --> 00:19:41.950
Rhodes James himself was a conservative MP
and the equally slighting reference to Martin

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:46.799
not knowing Churchill was a reference to Rhodes
James himself having been close to Brendan

00:19:46.799 --> 00:19:52.799
Bracken who introduced him to Churchill.
Rhodes James and I never saw eye to eye, mainly

00:19:52.799 --> 00:19:57.320
because he was virulently opposed to Margaret
Thatcher whom I personally regarded, as I

00:19:57.320 --> 00:20:02.880
still do today, as the savior of her country
and the greatest peacetime prime minister

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:09.880
of the century. Thank you. I never had much
time for Robert's judgment of people ever

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:17.330
since he was the principle officer in the
executive office of the Secretary General

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:23.890
of the United Nations with the Austrian diplomat
Kurt Waldheim. Despite being a World War II

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:30.230
historian Rhodes James never spotted the slight
gap in his boss' resume for the rather key

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:37.169
years of 1944 and 1945. During which it later
turned out that Waldheim hadn't been studying

00:20:37.169 --> 00:20:44.169
for a law degree in the University of Vienna
as he claimed but in fact had been an in the

00:20:44.230 --> 00:20:48.850
stationed in Croatia where he denied seeing
atrocities despite shootings of partisans

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:54.150
two hundred feet from his offices or having
personally ever witnessed any anti-Semitism

00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:59.289
despite his having personally approved leaflets
to the Soviets saying enough of the Jewish

00:20:59.289 --> 00:21:05.289
war, stop the killing, come over.
The real point rebutting Rhodes James' critique

00:21:05.289 --> 00:21:11.570
of Martin Gilbert is a deeper one than even
Edward Luttwak makes however. Martin was more

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:16.039
than capable of making moral judgments and
placing himself in his books when he wanted

00:21:16.039 --> 00:21:21.950
to as his books on Israel and the holocaust
proved but he recognized that the subjects

00:21:21.950 --> 00:21:27.750
he was writing about was for the ages, not
for an immediate time period. Any value judgments

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:33.289
he sough to impose on his great, multi-volume
biography were likely to be out of date within

00:21:33.289 --> 00:21:39.130
a few decades. By allowing Churchill to speak
to us in his own words rather than through

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:46.130
any authorial prism of Martin's, the biography
leaves us with what every reader wants and

00:21:46.559 --> 00:21:51.980
needs from a great biographer, the real subject
on his own.

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:57.419
That's required Martin to step back and allow
the great man to take all the limelight. Of

00:21:57.419 --> 00:22:02.190
course Martin was constantly using his historian's
judgment as to what to include and what to

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:05.870
exclude, and we was constantly shocked that
the biography was so short rather than so

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:11.350
long as we hear from Richard. So in that sense
he was imposing himself but at no point did

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:17.490
he allow vanity or self-importance to go down
Rhodes James' route of trying to make contemporaneous

00:22:17.490 --> 00:22:23.120
value judgments on long past contrivances.
It's the key to understanding why it is one

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:28.299
of the greatest biographies in the language
and moreover, in my view, the sheer accumulation

00:22:28.299 --> 00:22:34.370
of information and quotation meant that Martin
did indeed succeed in painting, quote, the

00:22:34.370 --> 00:22:38.970
portrait of a soul through his adventures
through life.

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:44.169
Martin's totally debilitating stroke which
led to his death in February came, in my view,

00:22:44.169 --> 00:22:49.490
partly as a result, and not just my view either,
by the way ladies and gentlemen, of the British

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:56.490
media's obsession with the Iraq war. The Chilcot
Inquiry on which he sat along with Sir Lawrence

00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:04.120
Freedman and the Judge Chilcot and two others,
was exhausting work for Martin at a time that

00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:10.110
he least needed it at an advanced age. It
was just too much. We've already had four

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:17.110
inquiries on the Iraq war. They've all said
the same thing, we didn't need to have a fifth.

00:23:19.210 --> 00:23:26.210
Unfortunately we did. Both Martin and Laurie
Freedman were attacked for even being on the

00:23:28.929 --> 00:23:34.820
committee because they were Jewish, an appalling
outbreak of anti-Semitism from amongst others.

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:41.330
Oliver Miles the former ambassador to Libya,
a classic example I'm afraid ladies and gentlemen

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:46.730
of the foreign office at it's absolute arabist
worst.

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:51.590
Biographers of Churchill left, right, good,
bad and indifferent are all facing a terrible

00:23:51.590 --> 00:23:56.370
encroaching ignorance about Churchill which
stems from the fact that his life and achievements

00:23:56.370 --> 00:24:00.140
are not presently taught about in school to
anything like the degree that they ought to

00:24:00.140 --> 00:24:06.100
be. As Richard pointed out in his speech,
in a recent survey twenty-three percent of

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:13.100
British teenagers thought that Churchill was
a fictional character. They also by the way

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:20.230
thought that Sherlock Holmes and Eleanor Rigby
were real people. However none of this is

00:24:22.130 --> 00:24:27.640
quite as bad as the forty percent of British
teenagers, and not a small number either,

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:33.380
they interviewed I think 1600 of them, forty
percent of them who believed that the American

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:38.090
War of Independence had been won by Denzel
Washington.

00:24:38.090 --> 00:24:45.090
Anyway, let's look for some good news. The
good news is that people are still very keen

00:24:47.980 --> 00:24:54.980
to learn about Churchill when they're given
the chance. Boris Johnson wrote that book

00:24:55.370 --> 00:25:02.370
the other day and he has sold 200,000 copies
of it. Actually my favorite review of it that

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:11.510
said the problem with Winston Churchill is
that he thinks he's Boris Johnson. I did my

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:17.779
best by the way ladies and gentlemen when
he sent me that book to try and get rid of

00:25:17.779 --> 00:25:23.010
the mistakes but even I wasn't able to stop
him from saying that Winston Churchill was

00:25:23.010 --> 00:25:30.010
camp. The least camp person it's possible
to think of in history must be Sir Winston

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:35.970
Churchill but nonetheless that's Boris.
There are good, readable biographies by Roy

00:25:35.970 --> 00:25:42.640
Jenkins, who didn't visit any archives and
he seemed to be under the impression that

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:48.390
Winston Churchill was a liberal all his life
but nonetheless it's immensely readable and

00:25:48.390 --> 00:25:55.059
the political bits are fun. But also by Elizabeth
Longford, Henry Pelling, Norman Rose, Jefferey

00:25:55.059 --> 00:26:01.010
Best and several others. Churchill also was
fortunate to have had a large number of serious

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:06.700
and scholarly figures, by no means all from
the academy. Many of the best actually have

00:26:06.700 --> 00:26:12.950
been enthusiasts from outside the academy
who've added immeasurably to our knowledge

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:16.120
of him.
In no particular order and conscious of missing

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:21.529
out dozens of others I'd like to mention Paul
Addison, on Churchill's domestic policies,

00:26:21.529 --> 00:26:28.529
John Ramsden on his legacy, David Reynolds
and Manfred Weidhorn on his writing, and Max

00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:33.950
Hastings, I don't always agree with everything
Max Hastings said but nonetheless he's worked

00:26:33.950 --> 00:26:38.039
impressively on his war craft. David Dilks
has been mentioned on his friendships and

00:26:38.039 --> 00:26:43.500
his relations with the commonwealth. Richard
Langworth of course who I see as Churchill's

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:50.500
representative on the earth. John Mather has
written about his health. Jonathan Schneer

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:57.289
on the war cabinet which is an aspect very
well covered years ago by Sheila Lawler as

00:26:57.289 --> 00:27:03.230
well. Lynn Olsen on his youthful divertees.
Mary Soames of course, the late Mary Soams

00:27:03.230 --> 00:27:08.890
and the late Dick Howe on his marriage. John
and Celia Lee on his immediate family. Will

00:27:08.890 --> 00:27:14.020
Morrisey and Francois Cassidy on Churchill's
relationship with De Gaulle. Warren Kimball

00:27:14.020 --> 00:27:19.250
on his relationship with FDR. David Stafford
on intelligence. Con Coughlin on his time

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:26.100
with the Malakand Field Force. Raymond Callahan
and Barry Pitt and John Keegan on his relations

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:30.679
with his generals and Christa Labelle and
Stephen Roscoe on his relations with his admirals.

00:27:30.679 --> 00:27:37.679
There's been Barbara Leeming on his penultimate
decade. Celia Sands on his travels. Kenneth

00:27:38.330 --> 00:27:42.950
Weisbrode on his relationship with King George
the sixth. Morris Ashley and Bill Deekan on

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:48.539
his time as a historian. Peter Clarke on his
journalism. Cita Stelzer, who's with us in

00:27:48.539 --> 00:27:54.760
the audience today, on his eating and drinking.
Ted Morgan, Michael McCannalin 0and Kurt on

00:27:54.760 --> 00:28:01.350
his youth. Barry Singer of course on his sense
of style. Stephen on his cigars. Rodney Croft

00:28:01.350 --> 00:28:04.799
on his funeral and so on and so on and hopefully
so on forever.

00:28:04.799 --> 00:28:10.360
I suspect Warren Doctor will be joining the
honor roll with his forthcoming book on Churchill's

00:28:10.360 --> 00:28:17.039
relations with Islam as of course Larry Arnn
will be with his Churchill's trial and already

00:28:17.039 --> 00:28:22.169
is there with the work that he's been doing
on the official biography and to add to that

00:28:22.169 --> 00:28:27.529
role are also the bibliographies compiled
by Ronald Cohen and Kurt Zoller and the collections

00:28:27.529 --> 00:28:32.070
of essays by David Canadine and one edited
by Robert Blake and William Roger Lewis. Then

00:28:32.070 --> 00:28:38.049
there are the reissues of Churchill's books
by Jim Muller that we've just been hearing

00:28:38.049 --> 00:28:45.049
about, noble undertaking financed by in part
think ISI which is a great institution as

00:28:45.740 --> 00:28:49.450
well.
We historians can be a disputatious and sometimes

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:56.450
rather vain bunch. Not for nothing is the
collective noun for historians malice but

00:28:57.299 --> 00:29:02.809
most of us tweeling in the field of Churchill
studies have very conscious that we're only

00:29:02.809 --> 00:29:07.960
really dotting the I's and crossing the T's
of the great historians who've gone before,

00:29:07.960 --> 00:29:12.830
principally of course the person who many
of us here were proud to call our friend,

00:29:12.830 --> 00:29:19.830
the late, the great, Sir Martin Gilbert. Ladies
and gentlemen, thank you very much indeed.

00:29:23.669 --> 00:29:30.669
Female: Again we have some time for questions.
Dr. Roberts: This gentleman over there.

00:29:38.570 --> 00:29:45.570
Male 1: Could you tell me where you were and
what you were thinking in 2009 when the bust

00:29:47.000 --> 00:29:51.220
of Winston Churchill was removed from the
White House?

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:58.220
Dr. Roberts: This is an interesting issue
because there are some people who argue that

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:11.330
it wasn't given to America per se. It was
never expected to be kept in the Oval Office

00:30:11.710 --> 00:30:17.740
all the time, that it was only given to the
Bush administration and therefore the Obama

00:30:17.740 --> 00:30:24.740
administration had every right to get rid
of it. I frankly haven't looked into it in

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:31.510
anything like the degree that some others
have. I of course felt, like I think probably

00:30:31.510 --> 00:30:34.779
everybody in this room did, that it was a
great shame, that it should have been taken

00:30:34.779 --> 00:30:39.700
out of the Oval Office. I didn't know at the
time that there was another bust in the White

00:30:39.700 --> 00:30:46.220
House somewhere else and that it should have
been given back to the British Embassy, but

00:30:46.220 --> 00:30:53.220
the fact is that President Obama just has
every right to put busts Martin Luther King

00:30:53.799 --> 00:30:58.419
where ones of Winston Churchill were if he
believes, which he seems to, that his grandfather

00:30:58.419 --> 00:31:03.649
was tortured by Winston Churchill which he
was not.

00:31:03.649 --> 00:31:07.070
The president is under the impression, or
seems to have been, I don't know whether he's

00:31:07.070 --> 00:31:11.049
looked at any of this recent work that's been
done and the articles that have been written

00:31:11.049 --> 00:31:16.830
that have looked into this in some detail
and which prove beyond doubt, it seems to

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:23.830
me, that the dreams of his father are precisely
that, dreams. He seems to have been a deeply,

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:33.940
deeply dishonest person, President Obama's
father, who made up things all the time when

00:31:33.940 --> 00:31:40.940
he was sober and was in terms of what the
accidents that he caused and the damage that

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:49.909
he caused to people, it strikes me as a deeply
unpleasant person and if one of the lies that

00:31:49.909 --> 00:31:56.909
he made up was that his father, President
Obama's grandfather, was some great Myanmar

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:03.409
nationalist leader and liberation fighter.
Well I'm afraid that just fits in very much

00:32:03.409 --> 00:32:10.409
with the overall level of duplicity that that
man has shown.

00:32:13.190 --> 00:32:20.190
Male 2: Hello over here. Earlier you talked
about the difference and the change of the

00:32:24.769 --> 00:32:30.860
treatment of Winston Churchill by his biographers.
I was wondering if you find a parallel with

00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:36.450
that with today and Margaret Thatcher and
whether she's in danger of becoming mythical

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:43.450
in the foreseeable future?
Dr. Roberts: Well as an historian, the world

00:32:43.820 --> 00:32:50.620
mythical is very difficult. I mean she's legendary,
she'll always be legendary. Myth implies that

00:32:50.620 --> 00:32:56.929
the stories aren't necessarily true and having
know Margaret Thatcher, in fact she appointed

00:32:56.929 --> 00:33:01.919
me to take her place on the Margaret Thatcher
archive trust and she came around for dinner

00:33:01.919 --> 00:33:08.620
at our house we went round to her and she
was somebody who the more extraordinary the

00:33:08.620 --> 00:33:15.620
story, the more likely I am to believe it
in fact. So the myths about her, if you look

00:33:17.580 --> 00:33:24.580
at them closely, of course anybody who's a
strong leader like her will accrete like barnacles

00:33:27.409 --> 00:33:34.409
accreting on the bottom of a well sailed boat.
However the fact is that she was a bit like

00:33:37.760 --> 00:33:44.760
Winston Churchill, somebody who one told stories
about. She was a truly extraordinary and inspirational

00:33:46.409 --> 00:33:52.399
person. So I think with that it's a bit like
Martin Gilbert and Winston Churchill, you

00:33:52.399 --> 00:33:59.399
go to Charles Moore's excellent official biography
of her and see what he thinks.

00:33:59.590 --> 00:34:06.570
Male 2: I meant mythical as in the twenty-three
percent of British children find Winston Churchill

00:34:06.570 --> 00:34:09.129
mythical.
Dr. Roberts: I see yeah. In a hundred years

00:34:09.129 --> 00:34:12.849
time, I'm sure twenty-three percent of British
schoolchildren won't believe that Margaret

00:34:12.849 --> 00:34:19.530
Thatcher existed either.
Male 3: Should I raise my hand? Okay.

00:34:19.530 --> 00:34:25.919
Dr. Roberts: Well unless there are any other-
Male 3: Oh, I have one.

00:34:25.919 --> 00:34:29.339
Dr. Roberts: Oh, okay.
Male 3: You mentioned some of the biographies

00:34:29.339 --> 00:34:36.339
and I'm especially recently familiar with
the Jenkins biography. I think he summed it

00:34:36.349 --> 00:34:41.810
up by saying that he had previously though
Gladstone was the greatest Englishman of all

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:44.619
time and he changed his mind after doing his
research on Churchill.

00:34:44.619 --> 00:34:48.129
Dr. Roberts: To hold the position of Prime
Minister, is what he said.

00:34:48.129 --> 00:34:54.169
Male 3: Right. Exactly, the greatest Prime
Minister, right. What about William Manchester?

00:34:54.169 --> 00:34:56.649
You didn't talk about Manchester as a biographer.
I was wondering-

00:34:56.649 --> 00:34:59.540
Dr. Roberts: I didn't.
Male 3: ... your impression of his books.

00:34:59.540 --> 00:35:05.140
Dr. Roberts: I didn't. I'm afraid that probably
I'm the only person in this room that was

00:35:05.140 --> 00:35:12.140
actually disappointed by, not the literature
of William Manchester. He was a very good

00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:21.200
writer but it just didn't work for me. I don't
know why, maybe it was the ... I read it when

00:35:22.089 --> 00:35:29.089
I was university and I was expecting, maybe
I had high expectations of the historical

00:35:30.790 --> 00:35:35.150
scholarshop. I'm afraid I think the third
volume shouldn't have been published at all.

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:40.609
It was pitted with errors and was unfortunately
... although it was written by a very nice

00:35:40.609 --> 00:35:47.609
man, journalist, he hadn't covered any of
the scholarship that's been done in the last

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:56.380
twenty years since William Manchester had
his stroke and it just ... Don't worry. I

00:35:57.670 --> 00:36:04.040
know William Manchester's written these fabulous
selling books but I didn't put him down on

00:36:04.040 --> 00:36:11.040
that list for a conscious reason.
Male 4: Dr.Roberts, just to digress a little

00:36:11.619 --> 00:36:17.000
bit, what are you currently working on and
what can we look forward to in your next work?

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:22.780
Dr. Roberts: Well don't hold your breath because
it's not going to be published until 2018.

00:36:22.780 --> 00:36:27.570
Which for me by the way is absolutely nothing.
My last book on Napoleon took me longer than

00:36:27.570 --> 00:36:33.810
Napoleon spent on Centilina and Elba put together,
but no, it's a single volume biography of

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:40.070
Churchill. I've been commissioned by Penguin
US and UK to write a quarter of a million

00:36:40.070 --> 00:36:46.619
word, cradle to the grave life of the great
man so that's how I'm going to be spending

00:36:46.619 --> 00:36:53.619
my next three or four years. Hugely looking
forward to them. I'm already deep into the

00:36:54.910 --> 00:36:59.619
second volume of his speeches. You know he
spoke eight million words I think it was so

00:36:59.619 --> 00:37:06.619
that's what I'm going to be doing and a bit
like Richard and Jim said, the more you read

00:37:09.990 --> 00:37:16.030
of his his own words and that's especially
true of the speeches of course by you also

00:37:16.030 --> 00:37:22.339
have the witty, we're joined in the house
of commons in those speech books. The more

00:37:22.339 --> 00:37:28.650
you realize that Martin Gilbert was so right
when he said that he'd only managed to put

00:37:28.650 --> 00:37:35.650
ten percent of the whole in to the great official
biography which is another reason why the

00:37:39.990 --> 00:37:46.990
end of writing biographies will always be
more and all of us could really spend the

00:37:47.300 --> 00:37:53.599
rest of our lives reading everything that
Winston Churchill ever said or wrote.

00:37:53.599 --> 00:37:59.089
We'd still be holding our sides with laughter
at some of his witty adjoined and brilliant

00:37:59.089 --> 00:38:04.810
epithet. I'm just going to finish with one
which Richard will probably tell me he never

00:38:04.810 --> 00:38:11.810
said. Only tell me afterwards please, rather
than before the punch line Richard. When he

00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:19.920
spoke, as you all know, he was continually
broke. As we heard from Barry he spent more

00:38:20.310 --> 00:38:26.270
money on champagne than he should have. Twenty-six
pounds, eighteen shillings in 1899. Can you

00:38:26.270 --> 00:38:31.470
imagine how much money that was? I mean that
was the same as an average person earned in

00:38:31.470 --> 00:38:38.020
those days and so he was constantly broke
or on the verge of being broke and way he

00:38:38.020 --> 00:38:45.020
made his money was to come out to America
and to give speeches to enormous numbers of

00:38:45.660 --> 00:38:52.660
people across the country. During the Indian
Constitutional Crisis he was heckled by a

00:38:53.470 --> 00:38:58.950
lady who shouted at him from the back. "So
Mr. Churchill what do you intend to do about

00:38:58.950 --> 00:39:03.200
your Indians?" "Least ways madame," he replied,
"not what you did with yours." Thank you so

00:39:03.200 --> 00:39:04.359
much.
Tim: Well thank you very much Andrew and thanks

00:39:04.359 --> 00:39:05.970
all of you for attending this Hillsdale College
National Leadership Seminar. We are adjourned.

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:06.220
Thank you.

